===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*krey-}}
{{prefix|en|de|criminalization}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|dēkrĭ"mĭnəlīzā'shən}}, {{IPA|en|/diːˌkɹɪmɪnəlaɪzˈeɪʃən/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# The act of making an activity or substance no longer [[criminalized]] (no longer a [[crime]], subject to criminal penalties, to perform or possess). {{q|Compare {{m|en|legalization}}, which often implies [[regulation]].}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''decriminalization''' of all plant-based psychedelics}}
# The act of ceasing to treat a person as a [[criminal]], by making their activities [[legal]] or no longer subjecting them to the {{w|criminal justice system}} (but instead possibly the mental healthcare system, etc).
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2006|author=Mark R. Munetz|author2=Patricia M. Griffin|chapter=Use of the Sequential Intercept Model as an approach to '''decriminalization''' of people with serious mental illness|title=Psychiatric Services|section=57(4), 544-549}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Sverre Molland|title=The Perfect Business? Anti-Trafficking and the Sex Trade along the Mekong|publisher=University of Hawaii Press|isbn=9780824865825|page=38
|passage=Most sex work feminists support either the legalization or the '''decriminalization''' of sex workers.}}

